  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nLate in the week, Tropical Storm Isaias is forecast to stay quite \ndistant from our area, and direct impacts to our area will be \nnegligible. Those with weekend plans for the Texas coast should \nrefer to information from our neighboring NWS offices (CRP, HGX, and \nBRO) for further details regarding the risk of higher tides and rip \ncurrents.\n\nSouth Central Texas will stay within the dry envelope of the passing \ntrough into weekend, keeping conditions stable and dry. Daytime \ntemperatures will continue to be warm, in the mid 80s to mid 90s, \nwith overnight temperatures mostly in the 60s.\n\nBy late Sunday, the trough will clear our area, opening the door for \nmoist southerly flow. This is forecast to raise overnight \ntemperatures back into the low to mid 70s as muggier Gulf air \nreturns, while highs could see some slight increases into the low to \nmid 90s as warmer Mexican plateau air takes the place of the \noutgoing trough. This pattern is a little more conducive to diurnal \nconvection, and thus the forecast features isolated chances (10 to \n30 percent) for showers or storms during the afternoons. Along the \nPacific coast, a strong deep-layer trough will amplify this weekend \ninto early next week, contributing to the southerly flow over our \narea. There is good model agreement in moisture from Tropical \nDepression Twenty-E in the Pacific eventually being caught up in \nthis flow, but whether this could implicate South Central Texas \nearly next week depends on whether the system tracks more west and \nmisses our area entirely, or if placement farther east allows \nmoisture to diffuse into our area. For now, both the GFS and ECMWF \nplace the moisture plume west of our region. Ridging is favored to \nstay over the area through midweek next week, so temperatures should \nremain near to warmer than average during that period.",
